= Solution
{parent=2}

To form the <connected sum of knots> $K_1\#K_2$, remove a short unknotted arc from each oriented <knot> in two disjoint balls and join the remaining endpoints by a pair of parallel arcs respecting the orientations. Equivalently, a <sphere> meeting a <knot> in two points cuts it into two one-string <tangles>; close each by an arc in the <sphere> to recover its summands. Sliding the small joining balls along the <knots> shows that the <isotopy> type does not depend on these choices. The <unknot> is the identity for this operation. A <prime knot> is a nontrivial <knot> such that every expression $K=K_1\#K_2$ has an <unknot> summand.

We first prove <additivity of Seifert genus>, which also supplies a terminating measure for decomposition. Joining minimal <Seifert surfaces> along their boundary arcs gives $g_s(K_1\#K_2)\leq g_s(K_1)+g_s(K_2)$. For the reverse inequality, let $F$ be a minimal-genus <Seifert surface> for the sum and $S$ a <splitting sphere of a knot>. Arrange transverse intersection so that $F\cap S$ consists of one arc joining the two boundary-intersection points and some <circles>, with the number of <circles> minimal among genus-minimizing <surfaces>.

Each <circle> separates the <sphere> into two disks; since it is disjoint from the intersection arc, one disk contains neither marked endpoint. Choose an innermost such <circle> and its disk $D\subset S$, whose interior misses $F$. If its boundary were essential in $F$, compressing $F$ along $D$ would lower the genus of its component with boundary K: for a nonseparating curve genus drops by one, and for a separating essential curve the removed boundaryless side has positive genus. This contradicts minimal genus. Its boundary therefore bounds a disk in $F$. Replace that disk by a small push-off of $D$, removing the intersection <circle> without changing genus or boundary, again contradicting minimal intersection. Thus no <circles> remain.

Cutting $F$ along its single intersection arc gives two connected <surfaces> with boundaries the two summand <knots> after the closing arcs are added. <Euler characteristic> shows that their genera add to $g(F)$. Each genus is at least the minimum genus of its summand, so
$$
\boxed{g_s(K_1\#K_2)=g_s(K_1)+g_s(K_2).}
$$
Genus zero means that the <knot> bounds a disk and is the <unknot>. Every nontrivial <knot> therefore has positive genus. Induct on this nonnegative integer: a nontrivial nonprime <knot> splits into two nontrivial summands, each with strictly smaller genus by the equality above, and induction decomposes both into primes. This proves \b[every <knot> is a finite connected sum of <prime knots>], with the <unknot> the empty sum. Uniqueness is not needed here.

For coprime integers p,q, define the <torus knot> as the oriented parametrized curve
$$
T_{p,q}(s)=2^{-1/2}(e^{ips},e^{iqs})\subset S^3\subset\mathbb C^2,\qquad s\in\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z.
$$
Coprimality makes it an embedded single <circle>: two parameters with both coordinate phases equal differ by a multiple of $2\pi$. It lies on the standard unknotted <torus> separating the two <solid tori>. Negative exponents change the slope/<orientation> convention; they are allowed. If an exponent is zero, coprimality forces the other to be plus or minus one, giving an <unknot>.

Assume now $|p|,|q|\geq2$. We give a group-theoretic proof that <torus knots are prime>, making the peripheral ingredient explicit. The <Seifert-van Kampen theorem> applied to the two solid-torus sides of the exterior, joined along the annulus parallel to the <knot>, gives
$$
G=\langle a,b\mid a^p=b^q\rangle.
$$
Here a and b are the two core loops. The element $z=a^p=b^q$ is central. Under the <abelianization> $G\to\mathbb Z$, choose $a\mapsto q$, $b\mapsto p$; these generate the target because p,q are coprime. Thus $z\mapsto pq\ne0$, so z is nontrivial. A meridian has, up to conjugacy, the form $m=a^ub^v$ with $qu+pv=1$. This follows by tracing the meridional disk across the two <torus> sides, using a dual primitive slope intersecting the <knot> slope once. Different Bezout solutions change u by a multiple of p and v by the opposite multiple of q, leaving the same word because z is central.

Quotienting by z gives $G/\langle z\rangle=C_{|p|}*C_{|q|}$. Neither u nor v is zero modulo the corresponding cyclic order, by $qu+pv=1$. Hence the image of m is a cyclically reduced word of two syllables. All its nonzero powers remain reduced, so it has infinite order. In particular G is nonabelian and the <knot> is not an <unknot>.

We use the <Loop theorem> in its usual form: a noninjective boundary fundamental-group map of a three-manifold supplies an embedded compression disk. For a nontrivial <knot>, its boundary <torus> is incompressible. To see why, every <sphere> in its exterior bounds a ball on the side not containing the <knot>, by the <Schoenflies theorem>. A compression of the boundary <torus> would produce a <sphere> and make the exterior a solid <torus>. The compression disk has zero <homology> class in the exterior, so its boundary is the preferred longitude; attaching the annulus from that longitude to the <knot> gives a spanning disk and an <unknot>. This proves that <nontrivial knot exteriors have incompressible boundary>. Their peripheral <groups> inject as $\mathbb Z^2$, so their <groups> strictly contain the cyclic meridian subgroup.

If the <torus knot> were a connected sum of two nontrivial <knots>, van Kampen on the decomposing annulus would give $G=G_1*_{\langle m\rangle}G_2$, with the meridian subgroup proper in each factor. The <center of a proper amalgam lies in the amalgamated subgroup>. One proof uses its <Bass-Serre tree>: a central elliptic element fixes a vertex orbit and its convex hull, which is the whole tree; a central translation would force the minimal tree to be its invariant axis, but a nontrivial vertex reflection reverses that translation. Thus a central element fixes every edge and lies in the edge subgroup.

The central element z would therefore equal $m^k$. Its image in $G/\langle z\rangle$ would give $\overline m^k=1$, forcing $k=0$ because that image has infinite order. This contradicts $z\ne1$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{T_{p,q}\text{ is prime whenever }\gcd(p,q)=1,\quad |p|,|q|\geq2.}
$$

